s a specific pattern in one suit. I don't have enough tiles in one suit to form that.\n\n- Pure Straight: Three chows in the same suit. Let's check my W tiles: W1, W3, W7x2, W8. Maybe I can form chows here. For example, W1-W2-W3 (but I don't have W2), or W3-W4-W5 (no W4, W5?), or W7-W8-W9 (no W9). Not enough for chows.\n\n- Mixed Straight: Three chows in different suits. Let's see. I have T2, T5x2, B1, B2, B9. Maybe T2-T3-T4 (no T3, T4?), B1-B2-B3 (no B3?), and others. Doesn't seem to fit.\n\n- All Fives: All melds and pair are fives. I have T5x2, but other tiles are not fives. So no.\n\n- Pure Terminal Chows: Not sure. Maybe not applicable.\n\nWait, maybe I can form a Chicken Hand. Chicken Hand is when you have a hand that would otherwise be zero fans. But since I need at least 8 points, that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a hand with multiple pairs and some pungs. Let's see. For example, if I discard a tile to form a pair and then hope to draw the needed tile.\n\nLooking at my current hand, if I discard one of the T5s, I can have a pair of T5s. Then, maybe I can form another pair. But I need four pungs and a pair for a standard hand, but with points. Alternatively, maybe I can form a hand with three pungs and a pair, but that's not enough points.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for a concealed hand with multiple pairs and some pungs. For example, if I have two pairs and two pungs, but that's not enough.\n\nWait, maybe I can form a hand with two pungs and two pairs. Let's see. For example, if I have two pairs and two pungs, but that's not a standard pattern. Maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can aim for a hand with multiple pairs and a pung. For example, if I have a pung of W7s (but I only have two), so no. Maybe a pair of W7s and a pair of T5s, then other pairs. But I don't have enough tiles.\n\nHmm. Maybe the best approach is to discard a tile that's least useful and wait for a self-draw. Let's see which tile to discard.\n\nLooking at my 