t have enough info.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, the safest bet is to discard a tile that is not part of any potential chow or pung. For example, W4. Since I have two, and there are four left, discarding one might be safe. Also, if I keep one W4, maybe I can form a pung later. Alternatively, discarding B9, since it's a terminal and less useful for chows.\n\nBut which is better? Let's think. If I discard W4, I have one W4 left. If I can form a pung later, that would give me points. But if I discard B9, I have one B9 left, which could be a pair. Depending on the hand, maybe the pair is more useful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard a tile that is part of a possible chow. For example, T4. If I discard T4, maybe someone can use it for a chow. But I don't have other T tiles except T7. So maybe not.\n\nAnother thought: my hand has W1, W3, W4, W7, W8. If I can get W2, then I can form a chow of W1-W2-W3. Similarly, if I can get W5 or W6, maybe for other chows. But I don't have those. So maybe the best is to discard a tile that doesn't block my own potential.\n\nGiven all that, I think discarding W4 is a good move. It's a middle tile, and I have another one. Discarding it might not help others much, and if I can keep one W4, maybe it can be part of a pung or chow if I draw another.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard W4, as it allows maintaining a potential pung and reduces the risk of aiding opponents.\nReason: Discarding W4 (one of two in hand) is safe due to sufficient remaining W4 tiles (4 unshown). Keeping one W4 supports possible future pung formation. The hand lacks clear chows, so discarding a middle tile like W4 minimizes risk while preserving flexibility.\nAnswer: Play W4"}
