oncealed hand, so I want to keep as many options as possible. \n\nAlternatively, playing B1. Since the previous player has a B1 pung, they might not need it. But if I discard B1, they might already have all three. \n\nSo maybe B1 is safe. \n\nBut I need to choose between these. \n\nIf I play B2, and someone takes it, that's a problem. If I play F4, and someone takes it, that's also a problem. \n\nBut which is more likely? \n\nGiven that the unshown count for B2 is 3, and I have one, there are two left. If someone has two, they can Peng. \n\nFor F4, unshown count is 3, and I have two. So there is one left. If someone has two, they can Peng. \n\nIf I discard F4, there's a higher chance someone has two (since there's only one left). But if I discard B2, there are two left. \n\nHmm. Maybe playing B2 is safer. \n\nAlternatively, playing T4. The unshown count is 2. If I discard T4, someone might take it. The player across has a T3-T4-T2 chow (from Chi T3), so they might have T4 already. \n\nBut since they already have a T4 in their meld (from Chi T3, which is T2, T3, T4?), no, the meld is [T2, T3, T4], so they have that chow. So if I discard T4, they might not need it. \n\nSo playing T4 might be safe. \n\nBut in my hand, T4 is part of a possible chow if I had T3 and T5. But I don't have T3. \n\nSo maybe T4 is not useful to me. \n\nConsidering all this, perhaps the best move is to play B2, as it's part of a potential chow that I can't complete, and if discarded, it might help someone else. But since I don't have B3, it's better to get rid of it. \n\nAlternatively, playing F4 could help others form a pung. \n\nWait, if I play F4, and someone has two, they can Peng. Since I have two, and there are three left, it's possible. \n\nBut the seat wind is South, and the prevailing wind is North. If I have a pair of F4s, maybe I can form a pung later. \n\nBut since I need to discard, maybe it's better to play B2. \n\nSo the evaluation would be to play B2 because it's part of a poten